Transform the following supply chain scenario into a structured JSON configuration. A schema template follows the description:


This network is set up to make GPUs, with 8 upstream suppliers feeding 2 internal sites, the manufacturing-focused Assembly Hub and the Logistics Center warehouse. The raw material mix is glass_fiber, silicon_wafer, solder_paste, and ceramic_substrate. Two in-process builds support the finished item: each sensor_array uses 3 units of solder_paste and 1 unit of silicon_wafer, while each lens_assembly needs 5 units of glass_fiber, 3 units of ceramic_substrate, and 1 unit of silicon_wafer. The finished GPU itself is a fairly component-heavy build, consuming 1 unit of glass_fiber, 1 unit of lens_assembly, 2 units of ceramic_substrate, 2 units of solder_paste, 2 units of silicon_wafer, and 4 units of sensor_array. At the inventory level, glass_fiber is restocked in periodic lots ranging from 35316.0 units to 90136.0 units, with deliveries arriving every 16.0 to 24.0 hours; its holding cost is $6.419 per unit and stock is reviewed every 20 hours. Silicon_wafer is also replenished on a schedule, in fixed lots of 67381.0 units, with arrivals somewhere between 9.0 and 23.0 hours and a holding cost of $4.059 per unit, reviewed every 28 hours. Solder_paste follows a min-max style rule: when inventory drops below 2579.64 units, it is replenished up to 4207.82 units; holding runs at $5.938 per unit, shortage cost is $35.073 per unit, and review happens every 9 hours. Ceramic_substrate uses a similar threshold approach, reordering below 1406.14 units and restoring to 4956.57 units, with a shortage penalty of $36.868 per unit and a 21-hour review cycle. Starting stock is substantial for the internally used builds and finished goods, with 1326349 units of sensor_array on hand, 2288530 units of lens_assembly, and 1897292 units of GPU. Lens_assembly carries $7.71 per unit in holding cost, $13.239 per unit in shortage cost, and is checked every 1 hour, while GPU inventory is reviewed every 1 hour with a holding cost of $0.683 per unit. On sourcing, GlassCore Ltd. supplies glass_fiber with an exponential lead time averaging 20.0 hours, at $130.152 per unit, and payment terms between 52.0 and 88.0 days after delivery; shipments from GlassCore Ltd. take exactly 5.0 hours to reach Assembly Hub. ElectroParts is the alternate glass_fiber source, much cheaper at $31.103 per unit, with lead time averaging 7.0 hours, payment due 81.0 days after receipt, and capacity capped at 994935 units per order; its transfer to Assembly Hub takes 3.0 hours. For silicon_wafer, SiliconWorks delivers with an average lead time of 18.0 hours, charges $133.314 per unit, and offers payment in 32.0 to 71.0 days, while MetalFab Inc. works on an 8.0 to 21.0 hour lead time, charges $273.887 per unit, and is paid after 35.0 days; MetalFab Inc. shipments take 8.0 hours to transfer, while the SiliconWorks link is immediate. Solder_paste comes either from CarbonSupply Co., which has a fixed 28.0-hour lead time, costs $106.125 per unit, allows 8.0 to 14.0 days to pay, and can supply up to 211907 units per delivery, or from BatteryTech, which runs between 17.0 and 25.0 hours of lead time, charges $163.219 per unit, offers 23.0 to 25.0 day payment terms, and is limited to 330165 units; BatteryTech shipments take 9.0 hours to move to Assembly Hub, while CarbonSupply Co. transfers immediately. Ceramic_substrate is sourced from FastChip Corp. and ChipCo. FastChip Corp. has a constant 17.0-hour lead time, charges $358.371 per unit, is paid 52.0 days after delivery, and moves material in 8.0 to 10.0 hours. ChipCo, by contrast, has a fixed 11.0-hour lead time, a much lower cost of $51.423 per unit, payment due after 20.0 days, and immediate transfer into Assembly Hub. Production is centered at Assembly Hub, which manages GPU, sensor_array, ceramic_substrate, silicon_wafer, solder_paste, glass_fiber, and lens_assembly inventory. The key operation there is inspection, where glass_fiber, lens_assembly, ceramic_substrate, solder_paste, silicon_wafer, and sensor_array are combined into GPU output. Each inspection cycle takes a consistent 10.0 hours and requires the Quality Scanner. That scanner has capacity for 1 unit at a time, processes work with an exponential service time averaging 0.942 hours, stays up for 285.264 hours at a stretch, and when it fails, downtime averages 34.14 hours; running it costs $7.214 per hour. There is also a CNC Machine with capacity for 5 units, a fixed service time of 6.981 hours, and an operating cost of $12.48 per hour. Finished GPUs then move from Assembly Hub to Logistics Center with zero transfer time. On the demand side, Ross Associates places GPU orders every 23.0 to 47.0 hours, with demand typically around 891259.0 units and variability of about 169682.0 units. Customers there wait 15.0 to 21.0 hours for delivery, any unmet demand is backordered, the selling price is $1905.389 per unit, and cash is collected 34.0 to 88.0 days later. Samsung Electronics orders more aggressively, arriving every 15.0 to 53.0 hours with order sizes between 2516718.0 units and 4212546.0 units. Its delivery promise is exactly 8.0 hours, available units ship immediately and the remainder is backordered, the sales price is $997.832 per unit, and payment comes in 11.0 to 36.0 days. The model runs in hours over a 730-hour horizon.

