Build a supply chain simulation configuration based on the following description. The JSON schema is provided below for reference:


This setup centers on making laptops at Advanced Fab, supported by three incoming materials: ceramic_substrate, solder_paste, and silicon_wafer, with finished laptops then moving on to the Storage Facility. Each laptop consumes 3 units of solder_paste, 3 units of ceramic_substrate, and 4 units of silicon_wafer. Ceramic_substrate is sourced from GlassCore Ltd. on an as-needed basis, with a supplier lead time fixed at 12.0 days, a purchase cost of $310.274 per unit, and payment due 27.0 days after delivery; for that item, the shortage penalty is $9.942 per unit and stock is reviewed every 24 days. Solder_paste also follows a demand-triggered replenishment approach and comes from GlobalComponents, which delivers in exactly 28.0 days at a cost of $473.528 per unit; payment terms vary between 13.0 days and 57.0 days, and the transfer from GlobalComponents to Advanced Fab takes between 4.0 days and 12.0 days. Silicon_wafer is handled differently, with periodic replenishment in batches averaging 7377.0 units and varying by about 1094.0 units, arriving on a regular schedule somewhere between 4.0 days and 18.0 days. MetalFab Inc. supplies that material with a lead time between 15.0 days and 25.0 days, charges $234.519 per unit, allows 79.0 days to pay, and can provide up to 294538 units per order; the silicon_wafer shortage cost is $6.693 per unit and its inventory is reviewed every 19 days. Production takes place at the manufacturing site Advanced Fab through an operation called testing, where solder_paste, ceramic_substrate, and silicon_wafer are converted into laptop output in a fixed 2.0-day cycle. Laptop inventory starts at 438214 units, and shortages on the finished item carry a cost of $10.583 per unit, with review happening every 1 day. The network also includes a Welding Station with capacity for 3 units, a constant service time of 8.362 days, and an operating cost of $18.316 per day; it stays up for 49.438 days at a time and, when it fails, downtime averages 47.936 days. On the demand side, Ross Associates buys the laptop, placing orders every 17.0 to 20.0 days, with demand centered around 3857325.0 units and varying by about 296292.0 units. Customer shipments take 14.0 days, and any unmet orders are backordered rather than lost. The laptop sells at $72.996 per unit, and customer payments are received between 21.0 days and 59.0 days later. The full model runs for a 500-day horizon with 1 replication.

