Generate a structured JSON configuration for the following supply chain description. A JSON schema is provided below for reference:


This network makes two finished goods, drone and router, using plastic_pellet and memory_chip as the incoming materials, with work split across Advanced Fab, Plant A, and a Logistics Center. The product structure is layered: each motor_assembly consumes 4 units of memory_chip and 3 units of plastic_pellet, while each cooling_unit uses 1 unit of plastic_pellet. A drone then requires 3 units of memory_chip, 1 unit of motor_assembly, and 4 units of cooling_unit, whereas a router is built from 2 units of plastic_pellet and 3 units of cooling_unit. Plastic_pellet is sourced on an as-needed basis, with inventory carrying cost of $9.523 per unit, shortage cost of $13.544 per unit, and stock reviewed every 6 hours. Memory_chip, by contrast, arrives on a scheduled cycle of exactly 74040.0 hours, with deliveries coming every 10.0 hours. On the supply side, FastChip Corp. provides plastic_pellet with an exponential lead time averaging 13.0 hours at $145.952 per unit, and invoices are paid between 15.0 hours and 29.0 hours after receipt. ElectroParts is another plastic_pellet source, charging $230.625 per unit, delivering in 12.0 to 17.0 hours, allowing net 59.0 hours for payment, and capping each order at 401975 units; shipments from ElectroParts to Advanced Fab take another 2.0 to 11.0 hours in transit. For memory_chip, BatteryTech supplies at $72.0 per unit with a fixed 20.0-hour lead time and payment terms between 53.0 hours and 68.0 hours, and its route into Advanced Fab adds a 4.0-hour transfer. Memory Star serves as the alternate chip supplier, with lead time ranging from 8.0 hours to 27.0 hours, a unit cost of $469.835, payment due in 40.0 to 76.0 hours, a maximum order size of 611141 units, and an 11.0-hour transfer into Advanced Fab. Inside the network, Advanced Fab manages drone, motor_assembly, memory_chip, and cooling_unit inventory, and runs a polishing step that turns memory_chip, motor_assembly, and cooling_unit into drone in a fixed 2.0-hour cycle using the CNC Machine. That CNC Machine has capacity for 2 units, processes with a constant 6.692-hour service time, works in batches of 12 units, waits up to 4.588 hours to form a batch, and costs $16.063 per hour to operate. Plant A manages cooling_unit, plastic_pellet, and router inventory, and performs a cutting operation that converts plastic_pellet and cooling_unit into router in 16.0 to 23.0 hours, with no named equipment requirement. Starting stock is very heavy throughout the system: 2584791 units of motor_assembly, 1810534 units of cooling_unit, 2907862 units of drone, and 4453959 units of router. The motor_assembly stock carries $1.953 per unit in holding cost, $17.963 per unit in shortage cost, and is reviewed every 1 hour; cooling_unit carries $1.084 per unit and is also reviewed every 1 hour. Finished drone inventory costs $4.704 per unit to hold with a 1-hour review cycle, while router inventory costs $7.014 per unit with the same 1-hour review timing. Customer demand is concentrated in a few very large accounts. Lenovo Group buys drone, placing orders every 17.0 hours, with demand roughly 568981.0 units on average and variability of 134974.0 units; promised delivery runs between 9.0 hours and 18.0 hours, the selling price is $3441.602 per unit, payment comes in 22.0 to 42.0 hours, and if stock is unavailable the sale is lost. HP Enterprise also purchases drone, with exponentially spaced arrivals averaging 34.0 hours and a fixed order size of 4820262.0 units; delivery to that customer is consistently 12.0 hours, the selling price is $4232.969 per unit, payment is received in 50.0 to 77.0 hours, and shortages are also treated as lost sales. Broadcom Limited buys router, ordering somewhere between 35.0 hours and 41.0 hours apart, with demand between 860996.0 units and 1776248.0 units; customer lead time ranges from 15.0 hours to 25.0 hours, routers sell for $4352.244 per unit, payment is received exactly 18.0 hours later, and any available units ship immediately while the remainder is backordered. In terms of flow, all 4 suppliers connect into the 3 internal sites, drone moves from Advanced Fab to the Logistics Center, and router moves from Plant A to the Logistics Center. The model runs for a 100-hour horizon with 10 replications.

