This network revolves around a Manufacturing Site and a Storage Facility that together support production of router units and EV_battery units, using silicon_wafer, lithium_cell, glass_fiber, and steel_rod as incoming materials. The upstream supplier base is fairly broad: Memory Star, NanoMaterials Inc., and ChipCo all feed silicon_wafer into the Manufacturing Site; MetalFab Inc. and PolymerSource provide lithium_cell; PrecisionParts and Process Go. supply glass_fiber; and FastChip Corp. ships steel_rod. Silicon_wafer is sourced on an as-needed basis, with a shortage penalty of $21.383 per unit and stock reviewed every 15 weeks. Memory Star delivers with an exponential lead time averaging 4.0 weeks, charges $166.697 per unit, gives payment terms somewhere between 51.0 and 86.0 days after delivery, and its shipments take 11.0 weeks to reach the plant. NanoMaterials Inc. offers a consistent 27.0-week lead time at $137.045 per unit with payment due between 33.0 and 55.0 days. ChipCo is quicker at 5.0 weeks flat, charges $122.532 per unit, allows 8.0 to 16.0 days for payment, and can supply up to 728606 units per order. Lithium_cell is also replenished based on demand, carries a holding cost of $9.649 per unit, and is reviewed every 28 weeks. MetalFab Inc. supplies it in 13.0 to 16.0 weeks at $286.493 per unit, on net 52.0 days, with a maximum order size of 263924 units. PolymerSource is another source, with an exponential lead time averaging 26.0 weeks, a cost of $52.312 per unit, payment due in 20.0 to 31.0 days, capacity of 897512 units, and a 13.0-week transfer to the plant. Glass_fiber works differently: it arrives in scheduled deliveries every 20.0 weeks, with lot sizes centered around 73619.0 units and varying by about 8800.0 units. PrecisionParts supplies it with a 14.0 to 16.0 week lead time at $422.134 per unit, payment terms of 38.0 to 57.0 days, capacity of 227149 units, and transfer time between 3.0 and 5.0 weeks. Process Go. provides another lane for glass_fiber, with an exponential lead time averaging 10.0 weeks, a unit cost of $79.385, payment in 51.0 to 78.0 days, and a 5.0-week transfer. Steel_rod follows a min-max arrangement: when stock drops below 3984.66 units, it is replenished up to 7841.21 units. It has a holding cost of $9.128 per unit, a shortage cost of $40.413 per unit, and a 1-week review cadence. FastChip Corp. supplies steel_rod in exactly 13.0 weeks, charges $401.061 per unit, requires payment 21.0 days after receipt, and the move into the plant takes between 4.0 and 8.0 weeks. Inside the operation, some materials are first turned into sub-assemblies. A circuit_board uses 4 units of glass_fiber and 2 units of lithium_cell, while a frame_structure consumes 2 units of steel_rod, 4 units of silicon_wafer, 5 units of glass_fiber, and 2 units of lithium_cell. Inventory is already substantial for these in-process parts: 91069 units of circuit_board on hand and 2634957 units of frame_structure. Circuit_board is reviewed every 1 week and faces a shortage cost of $45.063 per unit, while frame_structure is also reviewed every 1 week and carries a holding cost of $8.139 per unit. Router assembly at the Manufacturing Site is tied to an operation called testing. Each router requires 1 unit of frame_structure, 3 units of steel_rod, 4 units of circuit_board, and 3 units of silicon_wafer. The cycle time ranges from 1.0 to 26.0 weeks, and it uses the Welding Station. That Welding Station has capacity for 2 units, works with an exponential service time averaging 8.269 weeks, and costs $9.483 per week to operate. The router stock position starts at 2251124 units, with a holding cost of $8.057 per unit, a shortage cost of $0.67 per unit, and review every 1 week. EV_battery production is also run at the Manufacturing Site, through a fabrication step that takes exactly 29.0 weeks per cycle and does not call for a named resource. Each EV_battery needs 4 units of silicon_wafer, 4 units of lithium_cell, 2 units of glass_fiber, 1 unit of circuit_board, and 3 units of frame_structure. Beginning inventory is 355809 units, holding cost is $1.728 per unit, shortage cost is $40.703 per unit, and reviews happen every 1 week. There is also Test Equipment in the system with capacity of 4 units, an exponential service time averaging 8.402 weeks, and an operating cost of $17.756 per week. It is not always available: uptime runs a consistent 495.854 weeks before a failure, and downtime then averages 18.577 weeks. Finished router units and EV_battery units move from the Manufacturing Site to the Storage Facility for downstream fulfillment. On the demand side, Broadcom Limited buys router units every 9.0 weeks, with demand centered around 3620714.0 units and varying by about 5487.0 units per order cycle. Customers wait if product is not available, so unmet router demand is backordered. Broadcom expects delivery in 16.0 to 17.0 weeks, pays $3701.021 per unit, and settles invoices 63.0 days later. Bosch Automotive purchases EV_battery units with arrivals somewhere between 17.0 and 33.0 weeks, and order size is roughly 3332310.0 units with variability of 20218.0 units. Its orders are also backordered when stock is short, customer lead time falls between 9.0 and 24.0 weeks, the selling price is $3444.927 per unit, and payment comes 16.0 days after delivery. Dell Technologies is the other EV_battery customer, placing orders between 10.0 and 46.0 weeks apart, with demand anywhere from 2014109.0 to 3072460.0 units. Dell’s delivery promise is a fixed 25.0 weeks, the product sells at $557.488 per unit, and payment arrives 26.0 days later. If EV_battery inventory is unavailable for Dell, the sale is lost rather than queued. The model runs in weeks over a 730-week horizon, with the first 30 weeks excluded from results.

