This setup revolves around two finished goods, EV_battery and industrial_sensor, made from glass_fiber, microprocessor, and a manufactured sub-component called frame_structure. The frame_structure itself uses 1 unit of microprocessor and 4 units of glass_fiber, while each EV_battery requires 2 units of microprocessor plus 3 units of frame_structure, and each industrial_sensor consumes 1 unit of glass_fiber, 3 units of frame_structure, and 3 units of microprocessor. On the supply side, ElectroParts provides glass_fiber to Main Factory with a consistent 20.0-week lead time at $4.221 per unit, and payment is due 21.0 days after delivery. Glass_fiber is replenished on a regular schedule every 15.0 weeks, with shipment quantities centered around 72260.0 units and varying by about 16024.0 units; it carries a holding cost of $3.042 per unit and is reviewed every 17 weeks. BatteryTech supplies microprocessor to Main Factory at $169.714 per unit, with lead times ranging from 13.0 to 18.0 weeks and net 66.0 days payment terms. Microprocessor deliveries arrive every 1.0 week in fixed lots of 59091.0 units. Manufacturing is handled at Main Factory, which manages microprocessor, frame_structure, EV_battery, glass_fiber, and industrial_sensor inventory, while Warehouse serves as the storage and distribution point for finished goods. The cutting step at Main Factory converts microprocessor and frame_structure into EV_battery in anywhere from 8.0 to 10.0 weeks, and fabrication turns glass_fiber, frame_structure, and microprocessor into industrial_sensor in 10.0 to 28.0 weeks. Starting stock is substantial: 4854490 units of frame_structure, 4230732 units of EV_battery, and 1697299 units of industrial_sensor. Frame_structure has a holding cost of $8.567 per unit with a 1-week review cycle; EV_battery carries $6.05 per unit in holding cost, a shortage penalty of $8.698 per unit, and is also reviewed every 1 week; industrial_sensor is reviewed every 1 week with a shortage cost of $25.637 per unit. Customer demand comes from Samsung Electronics for EV_battery and NXP Semiconductors for industrial_sensor. Samsung Electronics places orders averaging every 50.0 weeks, with demand roughly 1119731.0 units and variability of about 139754.0 units; delivery commitments run between 14.0 and 25.0 weeks, the selling price is $4774.681 per unit, payment comes 22.0 days after delivery, and available units ship immediately while the remainder is backordered. NXP Semiconductors orders industrial_sensor with arrivals averaging 28.0 weeks apart, in volumes anywhere from 1414188.0 to 3211396.0 units; customer lead time falls between 15.0 and 21.0 weeks, the sales price is $4505.835 per unit, payment is received between 21.0 and 45.0 days later, and the business follows a partial-fill policy where shipped stock goes out and the unmet balance is lost. In network terms, ElectroParts and BatteryTech feed Main Factory, and Main Factory sends both EV_battery and industrial_sensor onward to Warehouse. The model runs over a 500-week planning horizon with 10 replications.

