This network revolves around two finished items, GPU and camera, produced through the Integration Center and then moved into the Storage Facility before serving customers. On the supply side, Process Go. feeds gold_wire into the Integration Center, while GlobalComponents sends in solder_paste. The gold_wire stream arrives on a regular 2.0-week schedule, with shipment quantities centered around 84571.0 units and varying by about 7998.0 units. Process Go. itself operates with an exponential lead time averaging 4.0 weeks, charges $202.424 per unit, and is paid 22.0 days after delivery. Solder_paste is replenished in fixed lots of 61900.0 units every 18.0 weeks. GlobalComponents typically delivers with an exponential lead time averaging 1.0 weeks, charges $355.709 per unit, and offers 70.0-day payment terms. Solder_paste is also tracked with a holding cost of $2.518 per unit, a shortage cost of $18.218 per unit, and a review cadence of 28 weeks. At the Integration Center, GPU production uses solder_paste, with 4 units of solder_paste needed for each GPU, and the cutting step takes exactly 4.0 weeks per cycle. Camera output is made from gold_wire, also at 4 units of gold_wire per camera, and the inspection step runs anywhere from 15.0 to 26.0 weeks. Finished stock starts at 425276 units for GPU and 1707826 units for camera. GPU inventory carries a holding cost of $4.899 per unit, a shortage penalty of $28.438 per unit, and is reviewed every 1 week, while camera inventory carries $2.561 per unit in holding cost, $40.205 per unit in shortage cost, and the same 1-week review pattern. The operation also includes a Laser Cutter with capacity for 2 units, a fixed service time of 0.22 weeks, uptime averaging 95.604 weeks, downtime averaging 5.934 weeks, and an operating cost of $9.544 per week. On the demand side, NXP Semiconductors buys GPU with orders arriving at an exponential average of 31.0 weeks, and order sizes are around 1296388.0 units on average with variability of 305260.0 units. GPU shipments to NXP Semiconductors take 3.0 weeks, any shortfall is backordered, the selling price is $3965.338 per unit, and cash is collected 87.0 days later. Lenovo Group buys camera on a steady 12.0-week cycle, each order being exactly 3041821.0 units. Delivery to Lenovo Group takes 19.0 weeks, shortages are also backordered, the camera sells at $2619.629 per unit, and payment comes in after 75.0 days. Structurally, the node setup links Process Go. and GlobalComponents with the Integration Center and the Storage Facility, and material flows run from both suppliers into the Integration Center, then from the Integration Center to the Storage Facility for both GPU and camera. The model is run in weeks over a 500-week horizon with 10 replications.

