hour payment terms, and can supply up to 922009 units per order; transfer to the Fab takes between 7.0 hours and 12.0 hours. CarbonSupply Co. handles ceramic_substrate with a steady 23.0-hour lead time at $84.016 per unit, and payment falls somewhere between 26.0 hours and 63.0 hours after receipt. MetalFab Inc. is the alternate ceramic_substrate source, with an exponential lead time averaging 13.0 hours, a higher price of $283.899 per unit, 76.0-hour payment terms, and a maximum order size of 204663 units. NanoMaterials Inc. also sells glass_fiber, typically arriving in about 14.0 hours, at $366.375 per unit and with payment due after 24.0 hours. Memory Star supports gold_wire supply with an exponential lead time averaging 20.0 hours, a unit cost of $419.351, payment terms between 24.0 hours and 77.0 hours, and capacity capped at 629903 units. FastChip Corp. adds another glass_fiber lane, with lead times varying uniformly between 9.0 hours and 21.0 hours, a cost of $405.889 per unit, and payment terms ranging from 46.0 hours to 80.0 hours. Inside the Fab, tablet production runs through a molding step using gold_wire, sensor_array, motor_assembly, and glass_fiber as inputs, producing tablet output in a cycle that ranges from 16.0 hours to 24.0 hours. That operation depends on a Conveyor Belt with capacity for 1 unit at a time and a service time fixed at 0.157 hours. The belt is batched, so work moves in lots of 12 units with a maximum wait of 17.149 hours before release. It also experiences breakdown behavior: uptime is a consistent 366.901 hours, downtime is 35.357 hours, and operating expense runs at $2.615 per hour. Router production at the same Fab goes through inspection, converting ceramic_substrate and glass_fiber into router units in exactly 16.0 hours. FPGA output comes from a cutting step, where motor_assembly and gold_wire are processed into FPGA units in anywhere from 14.0 hours to 22.0 hours. Finished goods then move from the Fab to the Logistics Center