 9.0 hours and 23.0 hours. The cutting operation turns gold_wire into router, with a cycle time ranging from 10.0 hours to 20.0 hours. The operation is supported by a Robot Arm resource with capacity for 5 units, an exponential service time averaging 5.759 hours, and an operating cost of $14.172 per hour. Reliability is not perfect: the Robot Arm runs for an average of 421.293 hours before failure, then stays down for exactly 19.437 hours each time. On the finished goods side, camera starts with 362517 units in stock, carries a holding cost of $7.979 per unit, incurs a shortage cost of $4.377 per unit, and is reviewed every 1 hour. Router begins much higher at 3029530 units, with a holding cost of $0.764 per unit and a 1-hour review cycle. Demand comes from two major customers. Siemens Digital buys camera, placing orders every 57.0 hours, with order sizes between 997404.0 units and 2668357.0 units. Camera shipments to Siemens Digital take exactly 28.0 hours, sell at $332.928 per unit, and follow a partial-fill lost-sales approach, meaning available units are shipped and any remaining demand is lost; payment is received between 45.0 hours and 72.0 hours later. Dell Technologies purchases router every 31.0 hours, demanding between 855635.0 units and 1799332.0 units per order. Router delivery to Dell Technologies takes between 15.0 hours and 24.0 hours, sells at $2914.38 per unit, and uses a backorder policy so customers wait for replenishment if stock is short; payment comes exactly 21.0 hours after delivery. The model runs in hours over a 730-hour horizon with 1 replication.

