th payment due between 25.0 and 34.0 days. Silicon_wafer is also multi-sourced: PrecisionParts delivers in a 10.0 to 13.0 week range at $139.459 per unit with payment due between 18.0 and 78.0 days; PolymerSource arrives between 9.0 and 10.0 weeks at $70.045 per unit, paid in 27.0 to 74.0 days, and can supply up to 879412 units; GlobalComponents has an exponential lead time with a mean of 18.0 weeks, charges $28.782 per unit, offers 16.0-day payment, and can ship as much as 863500 units; NanoMaterials Inc. is the fastest option, averaging 1.0 week, but at $300.104 per unit, with payment due 62.0 days after delivery and supply limited to 651928 units. Manufacturing is concentrated at the Production Center. The fabrication step produces drone from motor_assembly and silicon_wafer, runs on a fixed 11.0-week cycle, and requires the Packaging Machine. That Packaging Machine has capacity for 3 units, works with an exponential service time averaging 1.807 weeks, and costs $12.136 per week to operate. FPGA is built through an assembly step using steel_rod and heat_sink, with cycle time varying uniformly between 3.0 and 15.0 weeks. EV_battery moves through a coating step from power_module, and that process takes exactly 17.0 weeks. The site also uses a Quality Scanner with capacity 2 units, a fixed service time of 4.679 weeks, operating cost of $7.046 per week, and a failure pattern of 250.953 weeks up followed by 39.229 weeks down. Material flow is straightforward: all 8 suppliers feed raw materials into the Production Center, and the Production Center sends the finished drone, FPGA, and EV_battery lines on to the Storage Facility. On the demand side, HP Enterprise buys drone with orders arriving somewhere between 9.0 and 42.0 weeks apart, in quantities between 683503.0 units and 3198017.0 units, expects delivery in 7.0 to 8.0 weeks, pays between 50.0 and 75.0 days later, and purchases at $2482.162 per unit; if stock is unavailable, the sale is lost. Ross Associates orders 