n, offering capacity for 2 units, a fixed 4.046-hour service time, batching of 18 units with a maximum wait of 16.549 hours, average uptime of 390.162 hours, average downtime of 46.027 hours, and an operating cost of $9.111 per hour. Finished cameras, SoC units, and laptops then move from the Integration Center to the Warehouse. On the demand side, Apple Supply buys camera on a steady pattern every 13.0 hours, requesting exactly 2692021.0 units each time. Orders are promised in 26.0 hours, sold at $633.888 per unit, and paid in 17.0 to 87.0 days; if inventory is short, available units ship and the remainder is lost. HP Enterprise orders SoC with arrivals averaging 55.0 hours apart, demand centered around 4659194.0 units with variability of 243625.0 units, a customer lead time of 15.0 hours, and a selling price of $1153.53 per unit. Payment comes in 36.0 to 41.0 days, and any unmet demand is simply lost. Ross Associates purchases laptop with order arrivals averaging 37.0 hours, order size ranging from 294682.0 units to 4073976.0 units, and delivery promised in 11.0 to 24.0 hours. Those sales bring in $1839.269 per unit, payment is received 76.0 days later, and shortages are backordered rather than abandoned. The model runs over a 365-hour horizon, with results collected after a warm-up period of 30 hours.

