strate, and solder_paste, and it takes a constant 15.0 days. The Integration Center is responsible for inventories including lithium_cell, GPU, and ceramic_substrate in one flow, drone, circuit_board, solder_paste, lens_assembly, and copper_wire in another, and ceramic_substrate, CPU, solder_paste, lens_assembly, and copper_wire in a third, before GPUs, drones, and CPUs are all sent onward to the Storage Facility. On the demand side, TechCorp buys GPU, placing orders every 27.0 to 49.0 days, with demand centered around 1628193.0 units and varying by about 414825.0 units. Customer delivery for GPU takes 6.0 days, the selling price is $2520.557 per unit, payment comes 55.0 days later, and any shortfall is handled by shipping available units immediately and backordering the rest. Broadcom Limited purchases drone on a tighter rhythm, with arrivals between 19.0 days and 29.0 days and a fixed order size of 3205316.0 units. Drone orders are delivered in 27.0 days, sold at $2827.148 per unit, and paid in anywhere from 15.0 days to 49.0 days; if inventory is short, available stock is shipped and the remainder is lost. Apple Supply takes CPU orders every 49.0 days, with demand averaging 3069926.0 units and fluctuating by about 841230.0 units. CPU shipments go out in 12.0 days, sell for $2770.164 per unit, and are paid in 22.0 to 55.0 days, with partial shipments allowed and the balance backordered. In structural terms, all 8 suppliers—BatteryTech, Memory Star, MegaSupply, FastChip Corp., PrecisionParts, Process Go., CarbonSupply Co., and NanoMaterials Inc.—feed the 2 facilities, Integration Center and Storage Facility. The model runs in days over a 100-day horizon, with results collected after a 50-day warm-up period and 1 replication.

