mic_substrate, 2 units of glass_fiber, and 4 units of heat_sink, whereas each industrial_sensor needs 2 units of battery_pack and 3 units of epoxy_compound. Inventory on hand starts high, which makes this feel like a scaled-up electronics operation rather than a small shop: heat_sink begins at 397604 units with a holding cost of $5.217 per unit and a 1-day review cycle, battery_pack starts at 1058411 units with holding at $0.947 per unit, shortage at $22.099 per unit, and review every 1 day, tablet opens at 4529638 units with holding cost $6.537 per unit, shortage cost $49.363 per unit, and 1-day review, and industrial_sensor starts at 3951187 units with holding at $2.893 per unit, shortage at $40.859 per unit, and the same 1-day review cadence. Production is centered in the Pilot Line. One polishing step there takes ceramic_substrate, glass_fiber, and heat_sink and turns them into tablet units in a cycle ranging from 5.0 to 8.0 days, and that step requires the Injection Molder. The Injection Molder has capacity for 2 units, works with an exponential service pattern averaging 5.914 days, runs in batches of 65 units, waits up to 12.717 days to form a batch, and is not perfectly reliable: uptime is a consistent 249.57 days, downtime averages 48.626 days, and operating expense is $16.42 per day. A separate molding step at the same Pilot Line converts battery_pack and epoxy_compound into industrial_sensor units, with each run taking between 15.0 and 28.0 days. The facility also manages inventory for heat_sink, glass_fiber, tablet, and ceramic_substrate in one manufacturing flow, and industrial_sensor, battery_pack, and epoxy_compound in another. Supporting this, the Test Equipment resource has capacity for 3 units, a service time between 3.442 and 8.718 days, and an operating cost of $12.33 per day. On the demand side, TechCorp places tablet orders every 12.0 days, each for 2002548.0 units, and customers accept delivery in 7.0 to 10.0 days. If stock is short, those tablet orders are backordered rather than lost. The selling price is $2853.937 per unit, and cash comes in 85.0 days after delivery. Samsung Electronics buys industrial_sensor units every 42.0 days in lots of 1383074.0 units, expects fulfillment in 15.0 to 28.0 days, and follows a lost-sales setup, so if inventory is unavailable the sale disappears. That product sells at $3073.732 per unit, with customer payment arriving 72.0 days later. Finished tablet and industrial_sensor output both move from the Pilot Line to the Distribution Center. The model runs for 200 days total, with results counted after a 50-day warm-up period.

