raging 2.442 hours, and an operating cost of $16.494 per hour. Integration Center manages lithium_cell, aluminum_sheet, motor_assembly, and tablet inventory and performs fabrication, turning motor_assembly, aluminum_sheet, and lithium_cell into tablet on a fixed 24.0-hour cycle. Regional Depot acts as the warehouse receiving SoC from Plant A and tablet from Integration Center. Starting stocks are quite large: 1412156 units of motor_assembly, 1971847 units of SoC, and 640978 units of tablet. Inventory economics differ by item, too: motor_assembly has a shortage cost of $21.292 per unit with review every 1 hour, SoC carries a shortage cost of $20.202 per unit with review every 1 hour, and tablet has a holding cost of $6.487 per unit with review every 1 hour. On the demand side, Ross Associates buys SoC, placing orders every 12.0 hours with demand roughly 1712194.0 units on average and variability of 430642.0 units; promised delivery runs between 16.0 and 22.0 hours, the selling price is $1542.671 per unit, customer payment comes between 10.0 and 42.0 days, and shortages are handled by shipping available units immediately and backordering the rest. Dell Technologies purchases tablet with order arrivals anywhere from 32.0 to 40.0 hours apart and demand between 2615964.0 and 2921298.0 units; delivery to the customer is a consistent 24.0 hours, the selling price is $461.982 per unit, payment is received in 9.0 to 77.0 days, and any unfilled demand is fully backordered. The modeled network explicitly links BatteryTech, MegaSupply, and FastChip Corp. to Plant A, and connects Plant A, Integration Center, and Regional Depot as the operating facilities. The scenario runs in hours over a 100-hour horizon, with results collected after a 50-hour warm-up period.

