ion cycle varying uniformly between 15.0 weeks and 18.0 weeks. The site manages all four inventories—industrial_sensor, circuit_board, ceramic_substrate, and steel_rod. A Quality Scanner supports operations with a capacity of 2 units, an exponential service time averaging 8.856 weeks, batching turned on at 16 units per batch, a maximum wait of 9.34 weeks before a batch is processed, and an operating cost of $7.001 per week. Once built, industrial_sensor units move from Assembly Hub to Logistics Center. Customer demand comes from two major accounts. Bosch Automotive places orders for industrial_sensor exactly every 9.0 weeks, with demand typically around 2549643.0 units and varying by about 579789.0 units. Its requested fulfillment lead time falls between 15.0 weeks and 17.0 weeks, the selling price is $2566.215 per unit, payment is received 49.0 weeks later, and under shortages the business ships whatever is available while the remaining demand is lost. Dell Technologies orders the same product somewhere between 22.0 weeks and 42.0 weeks apart, with a fixed order size of 1138192.0 units. Dell expects delivery in 11.0 to 12.0 weeks, pays $1480.452 per unit, and settles invoices 64.0 weeks after delivery; if stock is short, available units are shipped immediately and the balance is backordered.

