with invoices paid between 21.0 days and 56.0 days after receipt. Production at Fab uses an Assembly Line with a capacity of 2 units, and that resource has an exponential service time averaging 2.758 hours. It runs with batching switched on, so jobs are grouped into batches of 59 units and can wait up to 5.086 hours before release, while the line itself costs $3.995 per hour to operate. For laptop output, Fab performs a polishing step that takes anywhere from 8.0 hours to 21.0 hours and consumes power_module, plastic_pellet, and solder_paste, with the Assembly Line required for that work. For SoC output, the same Fab carries out welding, using solder_paste and power_module to make SoC in a steady 18.0-hour cycle. On the demand side, Infineon Technologies buys laptop units, placing orders every 24.0 to 30.0 hours with order sizes between 2748551.0 units and 4571760.0 units. Delivery to that customer takes 7.0 to 27.0 hours, the selling price is $448.681 per unit, payment comes in 57.0 days, and if stock is unavailable the sale is simply lost. Bosch Automotive purchases SoC, with demand arriving every 29.0 to 44.0 hours in quantities from 1852209.0 units to 4681693.0 units. Their delivery lead time is exactly 20.0 hours, the sales price is $3729.021 per unit, payment is received after 83.0 days, and any unmet volume is backordered rather than lost. In network terms, ChipCo, NanoMaterials Inc., and ElectroParts feed Fab with raw materials, and Fab sends both laptop and SoC downstream to the Regional Depot. The model runs in hours over a 365-hour horizon, with results collected after a 30-hour warm-up period, and includes 5 replications.

