 managed together for an assembly step that converts heat_sink into smartphone in 3.0 to 19.0 weeks and uses the Assembly Line resource. The same Plant A also runs a fabrication step for tablet, drawing on steel_rod, heat_sink, and ceramic_substrate and finishing in 3.0 to 23.0 weeks, plus a welding step that turns ceramic_substrate into industrial_sensor in 9.0 to 14.0 weeks. The Assembly Line has a capacity of 4 units, a fixed service time of 5.289 weeks, batching turned on at 75 units, and it will wait up to 2.354 weeks to build a batch; running that resource costs $9.627 per week. Inventory starts quite high in this scenario: 3261526 units of heat_sink, 1285202 units of smartphone, 4666425 units of tablet, and 3828775 units of industrial_sensor. Holding costs are $1.449 per unit for heat_sink, $6.573 per unit for smartphone, $1.863 per unit for tablet, and $2.145 per unit for industrial_sensor, with all four reviewed every 1 week; smartphone inventory also carries a shortage cost of $0.794 per unit. Customer demand is split across three big accounts. Sony Electronics buys smartphone, placing orders with exponentially spaced arrivals averaging 20.0 weeks, for a constant 3243687.0 units each time, and expects delivery in 3.0 to 9.0 weeks. Smartphones sell to Sony Electronics at $3397.931 per unit, customer payment comes in 22.0 to 50.0 days, and the fulfillment rule is partial shipment with lost remainder, so available units go out and any shortfall is lost. Dell Technologies orders tablet with exponentially timed arrivals averaging 45.0 weeks; demand is roughly 4624583.0 units with variability of 402467.0 units, delivery is a consistent 19.0 weeks, the selling price is $3670.926 per unit, payment is received 20.0 days later, and any unmet volume is backordered. Ross Associates takes industrial_sensor orders somewhere between every 12.0 and 31.0 weeks, with demand centered on 4050846.0 units and varying by about 258704.0 units; customer lead time ranges from 10.0 