This network centers on making two finished goods, EV_battery and laptop, using epoxy_compound, silicon_wafer, and gold_wire sourced from ChipCo, CarbonSupply Co., and ElectroParts, then moving output from the Processing Unit into the Storage Facility. Each EV_battery consumes 1 unit of epoxy_compound, 4 units of gold_wire, and 4 units of silicon_wafer, while each laptop uses 4 units of silicon_wafer. On the inbound side, epoxy_compound is bought on an as-needed basis, with a shortage penalty of $29.89 per unit and inventory reviewed every 5 hours. Silicon_wafer follows a min-max style replenishment rule: when stock drops below 4615.44 units, it is raised back up to 12718.33 units; it carries a holding cost of $5.538 per unit and is checked every 25 hours. Gold_wire is also sourced on demand, with a shortage cost of $41.201 per unit and a 10-hour review cycle. ChipCo supplies epoxy_compound with an exponential lead time averaging 10.0 hours, charges $482.476 per unit, allows payment between 44.0 and 50.0 days after delivery, and can provide up to 254447 units per order. CarbonSupply Co. delivers silicon_wafer with an exponential lead time averaging 14.0 hours, at $414.727 per unit, with payment terms ranging from 49.0 to 59.0 days, and a maximum order size of 270225 units. ElectroParts handles gold_wire with a lead time somewhere between 11.0 and 13.0 hours, a unit cost of $387.533, payment due between 16.0 and 50.0 days, and capacity of 589657 units per delivery. Manufacturing happens at the Processing Unit in two streams: a testing step converts epoxy_compound, gold_wire, and silicon_wafer into EV_battery in exactly 6.0 hours, and a polishing step turns silicon_wafer into laptop in anywhere from 1.0 to 16.0 hours. The Processing Unit manages silicon_wafer, gold_wire, EV_battery, and epoxy_compound for the battery flow, and laptop plus silicon_wafer for the laptop flow. Finished goods are then sent from the Processing Unit to the Storage Facility. Starting stock is 3869309 units of EV_battery, with a holding cost of $9.032 per unit, a shortage cost of $22.116 per unit, and review every 1 hour. Laptop begins with 1034276 units on hand, has a holding charge of $6.909 per unit, and is also reviewed every 1 hour. Customer demand comes from Apple Supply for EV_battery and Sony Electronics for laptop. Apple Supply places orders at intervals between 12.0 and 59.0 hours, requesting between 169066.0 and 2182801.0 units each time, expects delivery in 13.0 to 23.0 hours, pays $3630.504 per unit, and settles invoices between 16.0 and 82.0 days later; if stock is short, available units are shipped and the remainder is lost. Sony Electronics orders laptop every 29.0 to 39.0 hours, with order sizes from 2585692.0 to 3046491.0 units, accepts delivery in 15.0 to 20.0 hours, buys at $1037.266 per unit, and pays exactly 45.0 days after receipt; here too, partial shipments go out and any unmet balance is lost. The model runs for 200 hours overall, with the first 50 hours treated as warm-up, and 10 replications are included.

