 of 46.458 days, and it costs $0.966 per day to operate. Plant A handles frame_structure and solar_panel inventory and runs a polishing operation that converts frame_structure into solar_panel output in anywhere from 14.0 days to 22.0 days. Starting stock is substantial: 3021001 units of frame_structure, 4699717 units of CPU, and 717329 units of solar_panel. Holding frame_structure costs $0.626 per unit with a 1-day review cycle, while CPU inventory is also checked every 1 day and costs $6.382 per unit to carry. Solar_panel inventory is reviewed every 1 day and, instead of a holding figure here, the key penalty is a shortage cost of $36.875 per unit. Finished CPUs move from Fab to the Fulfillment Hub, and solar_panel output moves from Plant A to the Fulfillment Hub as well. On the demand side, TechCorp buys CPU units with customer orders arriving at an exponential average of 8.0 days. Order size is normally distributed around 1736129.0 units with variability of 152840.0 units. CPU shipments promise a 2.0-day customer lead time, sell at $543.167 per unit, and use a partial-fill lost-sales approach, meaning available units are shipped and the rest of the demand is lost. Cash from TechCorp comes in 61.0 days after delivery. Siemens Digital buys solar_panel units on a very regular cadence, with orders every 20.0 days for exactly 1329178.0 units. Those orders are delivered in 5.0 days, priced at $2643.431 per unit, and any unmet demand is backordered until inventory becomes available. Payment comes 9.0 days after delivery, with the stated range also fixed at 9.0 days to 9.0 days. The scenario runs for 730 days, with results taken after a 30-day warm-up period.

