s also substantial: industrial_sensor begins with 2323687 units, incurs $5.151 per unit in holding cost and $33.582 per unit in shortage cost, and is reviewed every 1 hour, while CPU starts at 3639632 units, has a shortage cost of $31.239 per unit, and is likewise checked every 1 hour. Production is split across two manufacturing activities at the Processing Unit. The cutting step turns copper_wire, resin, and steel_rod into industrial_sensor and runs on a fixed 12.0-hour cycle. The bonding step combines steel_rod with cooling_unit to make CPU, and that cycle varies uniformly between 3.0 hours and 25.0 hours. A Quality Scanner supports operations with capacity for 1 unit at a time, a service time between 3.375 hours and 5.576 hours, and an operating expense of $2.694 per hour. It is not perfectly available: uptime is a steady 250.524 hours, followed by 5.849 hours of downtime each time it fails. On the demand side, Bosch Automotive buys industrial_sensor, placing orders anywhere from every 7.0 hours to every 25.0 hours, with demand centered around 2097350.0 units and varying by about 291263.0 units. Customer lead time runs between 7.0 hours and 21.0 hours, the selling price is $4575.873 per unit, payment arrives between 13.0 days and 20.0 days, and the service rule is partial fulfillment with the remainder backordered. Broadcom Limited purchases CPU on a steadier rhythm, with orders every 30.0 hours flat and demand ranging from 1439936.0 units to 1734425.0 units. Its delivery promise is exactly 14.0 hours, the sales price is $4935.977 per unit, receivables come in exactly 66.0 days, and any unmet volume is fully backordered. Material flows from MegaSupply, Process Go., and Memory Star into the Processing Unit, and both industrial_sensor and CPU then move from the Processing Unit to the Fulfillment Hub for distribution. The model runs for 730 hours, with results collected after a warm-up period of 30 hours, across 10 replications.

