This network revolves around 2 raw inputs, resin and silicon_wafer, feeding 2 manufacturing sites and a downstream Distribution Center. One fabricated sub-component, the cooling_unit, is built from 3 units of resin and 1 unit of silicon_wafer, and that part is then consumed in the GPU line, where each GPU requires 3 units of cooling_unit plus another 3 units of resin. The smartphone stream is simpler, using 1 unit of silicon_wafer per smartphone. Resin is managed at Plant A with a min-max replenishment approach: once stock drops below 4959.93 units, it is brought back up to 8328.79 units. Its carrying cost is $8.251 per unit, and inventory is reviewed every 13 hours. Silicon_wafer, by contrast, is supplied on a schedule, with batch quantities centered around 84810.0 units and varying by about 11557.0 units, arriving every 25.0 hours. Starting stock is substantial for the in-process cooling_unit buffer at 4170057 units, for GPU inventory at 4958001 units, and for smartphone inventory at 2572528 units. GPU shortages are penalized at $11.556 per unit, with stock reviewed every 1 hour, while smartphone inventory carries a holding cost of $4.11 per unit, a shortage cost of $22.182 per unit, and the same 1-hour review cadence. On the supply side, NanoMaterials Inc. provides resin to Plant A with a fixed 18.0-hour lead time at a purchase cost of $463.621 per unit, and invoices are settled between 22.0 days and 67.0 days after receipt. SiliconWorks supplies silicon_wafer at $109.989 per unit, with lead times averaging 6.0 hours, payment terms ranging from 10.0 days to 69.0 days, and a maximum order size of 701598 units. Material from SiliconWorks to Plant A also has a 13.0-hour transfer time. Plant A manages resin, GPU, and cooling_unit inventory and runs a coating step that converts cooling_unit and resin into GPU in anywhere from 13.0 hours to 26.0 hours per cycle, using 1 Robot Arm. That Robot Arm has a capacity of 1 unit, works with a service time between 1.64 hours and 9.176 hours, and costs $15.709 per hour to operate. Assembly Hub manages smartphone and silicon_wafer stock and performs an inspection step that turns silicon_wafer into smartphone in exactly 21.0 hours, with no named resource attached. Finished GPUs move from Plant A to the Distribution Center, and smartphones move from Assembly Hub to the same Distribution Center. On the demand side, NXP Semiconductors places GPU orders every 12.0 hours, with demand typically around 1471231.0 units and variability of 257839.0 units. Delivery to that customer takes 22.0 hours, GPUs sell at $2543.435 per unit, and under the partial lost-sales rule, available units ship while the remainder is lost; customer payment comes in between 9.0 days and 86.0 days later. Lenovo Group buys smartphones with order arrivals averaging 26.0 hours, demand anywhere from 523834.0 units to 4948759.0 units, and a 4.0-hour customer lead time. Smartphones sell at $1981.549 per unit, follow the same partial-fulfillment-with-lost-remainder logic, and are paid for 75.0 days after delivery. The model runs for a horizon of 365 hours, with results collected after a warm-up period of 50 hours, across 10 replications.

